      "knowledge_point_id": "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $5{y}^{2}-40y=-76.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-8y+16$ is a perfect square.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+16=(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-40y & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-8y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}) & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-16] & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $80$ to both sides and then dividing by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80+80 & =-76+80 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\ |y-4| & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
